19. EXECUTION INSTRUCTIONS
Begin by inspecting the repository and identifying the actual technology stack.
Then execute the audit systematically.
Do not immediately generate a list of generic security recommendations.
Do not assume that grep results alone constitute a review.
Do not claim to have read files that were not inspected.
Do not fabricate call graphs, runtime results, test results, or vulnerable code paths.
Use targeted searches to discover candidates, then inspect the surrounding implementation and follow the complete call chain.
Maintain durable progress notes so the audit can resume if execution is interrupted.
Prioritize high-risk security boundaries, but continue through the entire defined scope.
If the repository is too large for a single execution, divide the work into bounded batches and preserve the audit ledger between batches.
Do not declare completion while unreviewed security-relevant files or unresolved high-risk execution paths remain.
Start now with repository reconnaissance, create the audit ledger, and proceed through the defined passes.
The final goal is not to produce the longest possible vulnerability list.
The goal is to produce the most complete, technically defensible inventory of authentication and authorization vulnerabilities that can be established from the repository and the available verification environment.
